{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## CORRECTION TO THIS TICKET'S CENTRAL NARRATIVE — the alarm was NOT silent, and Defect 2 was not why nobody was paged\n\nReplaying the recorded series against the new configuration for AC2 produced evidence that REFUTES this ticket's framing of Defect 2. Recording it before anyone closes this bug on the current story.\n\n### Config: no drift\n\n`root_disk_pressure` code and live agree exactly — statistic Maximum, period 300, evaluation_periods 3, datapoints_to_alarm 2, threshold 85, GreaterThanThreshold, treat_missing_data `breaching`, no dimensions. AlarmConfigurationUpdatedTimestamp 2026-09-02T11:30:46 PDT (the fix). ActionsEnabled true, alarm and OK actions both to `rebar-gerrit-alerts`.\n\n### The raw series differs from the 30-minute rollup quoted in the description\n\n145 datapoints at 300s. First 2026-09-01T13:00 PDT = 86. Last real datapoint **2026-09-02T01:40 PDT = 85.0** (next value 07:10 = 48, post-recovery). The 94 reading is at **23:40**, not 23:33. The last two real datapoints (01:35 = 85.0, 01:40 = 85.0) are **NOT breaching**, because the comparison operator is `>` 85, not `>=`.\n\n### The 01:45 OK transition was caused by REAL DATA, not by missing data\n\nWindow [01:30 = 90, 01:35 = 85, 01:40 = 85]: two of three real datapoints are at-or-below threshold, which satisfies the ALARM->OK rule on its own. `treat_missing_data` is IRRELEVANT to that transition. The description's reading — that the alarm 'went GREEN at the exact moment the host became unreachable' because silence was read as health — is not what the data shows. It went green because the disk genuinely reported 85.0 twice, one hundredth below the threshold.\n\nThe replay model is validated against real history: it predicts the OK at 01:45 (actual 01:45:10) and an ALARM at 22:30 (actual 22:32).\n\n### THE ALARM FIRED ELEVEN TIMES AND NOBODY ACTED\n\n`describe-alarm-history` shows 22 StateUpdate events across 09-01/09-02, including **11 OK->ALARM transitions on 2026-09-01 alone**: 04:21, 04:56, 05:29, 12:03, 18:54, 19:44, 19:55, 20:35, 21:25, 22:03, 22:32 PDT. Every one published to the SNS alerts topic with ActionsEnabled true. The final ALARM latched 22:32 -> 01:45:10 — **continuous ALARM for 3h33m before the 02:05 outage**, and that is true under the OLD configuration too.\n\nThis reconciles with error sweep #6's 'flapped OK->ALARM 8 times' (7 from 18:00, 8 from 12:03) and confirms the sweep was substantially right. The 'nobody was paged' framing in the description is wrong.\n\n### What the fix actually bought\n\nThe treat_missing_data flip is still a genuine correctness fix — it stops the alarm reading a dead publisher as healthy, and it keeps the alarm latched through an outage instead of self-clearing. Its measurable incremental contribution here is **+10 minutes**: under the new config the alarm re-enters ALARM at ~01:55:10 (window [01:40 ok, 01:45 missing->breaching, 01:50 missing->breaching]), which is 10 minutes before the 02:05 gate-down firing, versus never under the old config.\n\nBut +10 minutes is not the difference between a 5-hour outage and no outage. **3h33m of continuous, actioned, SNS-delivered ALARM was.**\n\n### AC2 verdict: NOT MET AS WRITTEN, and unsatisfiable in its current form\n\nAC2 asks for 'an alarm exists that would have fired on the 2026-09-01T23:33 94% datapoint'. There is no 23:33 datapoint, and at the 23:40 = 94 reading the alarm had ALREADY been in ALARM since 22:32 under both configurations. Nothing 'fires on' it. AC2 needs rewriting against what actually happened.\n\n### The implication I want on the record\n\nThe dominant cause of this outage was **alert fatigue**, not the missing-data inversion. An operator was paged 11 times in 24 hours by an alarm whose threshold the host sat above for hours, and the signal was normalised. A remediation that only fixes treat_missing_data and adds bounded ceilings does not address that, and this is the fifth incident in this chain. I am escalating the scoping question rather than rewriting a P1 incident ticket's ACs unilaterally."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788401200345769001,"uuid":"1447895a-14dd-42b2-be71-7cff24c1c932"}